# Mermaid in instructions

Choose a diagram when it explains a relationship more clearly than prose. Give nodes and transitions meaningful labels, and include visible notes for constraints the reader needs. Mermaid comments (`%%`) are hidden in rendered output; use them only for source-level rationale that labels cannot express.

Keep the diagram consistent with its surrounding instructions and current implementation. Check fence syntax and, when rendered output is part of the deliverable, render it to verify the result. A diagram supplements the owning procedure; it does not introduce a second set of rules.
